  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with seasonably warm days and cool \nnights will continue through Saturday. \n\nMid-level ridging accompanied by surface high pressure will dominate \nthe weather pattern through the first half of the weekend. This will \nsupport a period of dry, fair weather conditions. 925mb temperatures \nfall a degree or two from Thursday resulting in slightly cooler \ntemperatures Friday in the low 60s. Temperatures trend cooler into \nSaturday with 925mb temperatures falling to +5-6C yielding highs in \nthe upper 50s to low 60s. A window of clear skies tonight and Friday \nnight will support efficient radiational cooling, especially \ntonight. NBM guidance has been consistently struggling with the \nlarger diurnal swings in this pattern, so leveraged MOS/MAV \nguidance. Temperatures tonight likely fall into the mid 20s and 30s \nfor the interior and 40s near the coast. High-res guidance indicates \npotential for a batch of clouds to shift in after midnight which \nwould limit how much temperatures drop. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing confidence in significant rainfall, gusty \nwinds, and rough seas from a coastal storm brushing the southern New \nEngland coast late Sunday into Monday.\n\nConfidence continues to increase that southern New England will see \nimpacts late Sunday into Monday from a coastal storm sweeping north \nfrom Florida. As the system approaches, it's expected to intensify \nand bring a surge of tropical moisture, rough seas, and gusty winds. \nThere is still a high degree of uncertainty on the exact track the \nstorm could take, with the latest ensembles split on a pass SE of \nthe 40W/70N (ECMWF ensemble) or a pass closer to SE MA/Nantucket \n(GFS and Canadian ensembles)... although it is worth noting that AI \nensembles remain significantly further west and inland compared to \nthe rest of the guidance suite. Regardless... due to the influx of \ntropical moisture (PWATs approaching 300 percent of normal across \nall guidance), a soaking rainfall across eastern MA and RI of 1-3\" \nis not out of the question. Depending on the exact track the storm \ntakes, heaviest rainfall could set up anywhere from offshore to \nnorthern RI. A father inland track would also raise concerns for \nfresh water flooding and gustier winds, with the latest guidance \nindicating a 10 to 30 percent chance of Gale Force winds (34 \nknots/39 mph) across the far southeastern waters by early Monday. \nRegardless of storm track, rough seas are expected, with the \npotential for 10-15 foot seas across the outer waters by late Monday.\n\nAstronomical tides will also be increasing through the weekend, so \nwe're watching the potential for tidal flooding, especially if the \nstorm track further west and inland.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry & much colder weather follows Tue into Wed \nafternoon with highs in the upper 30s to the middle 40s. \n\nMuch colder air works into the region behind the departing coastal \nstorm as large high pressure builds in from the west Tue into at \nleast the first half of Wed. Dry but cold weather expected Tue into \nWed afternoon with highs only in the upper 30s to the middle 40s. A \nsignificant change from the very mild weather we have been \nexperiencing much of the month.",
